# shir_nodes/field_extract.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Extract fields from delimited text (lowered from `cut -dD -fF`).
#
# Backends: Perl split(), JS split(), C strtok, Rust split().nth()
# Fallback: sh2.fieldExtract(text, delimiter, fields, suppress, output_delim)

node FieldExtract
tag "FieldExtract"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field delimiter: string
field fields: vec_field_range
field suppress_no_delim: bool
field output_delimiter: optional_string
spec: TextOps#cut_keeps_empty_field, TextOps#field_or_empty_out_of_range
spec: TextOps#select_subset, TextOps#complement_lengths
spec: TextOps#splice_single, TextOps#splice_three
spec: TextOps#order_is_input_not_argument
spec: TextOps#range_is_neither_first_nor_prefix
spec: TextOps#output_delimiter_is_not_appended
spec: TextOps#select_all_keeps_every_field
# Modelled as "the N-th field of a literal-delimiter split, or nothing", 1-based
# like cut, keeping empty fields (a,,b with -f2 is the empty string). The
# `fields: vec_field_range` RANGES and `--complement` are now modelled too, in
# docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Param.thy, over the field list `split_on` of
# ShIR_TextOps_Compose: a selector is `FOne n` or `FRange a (Some b)` / `FRange a
# None` (`-fN`, `-fN-M`, `-fN-`), a selector LIST is `-f1,3`, and the selection
# is the filter of the field INDICES in increasing order, so
#   * `select_subset` — every selected field is a field of the input (nothing is
#     invented, nothing is duplicated);
#   * `select_all_range` — `FRange 0 None` keeps every field, and
#     `complement_lengths` — `--complement` (`select_not`) plus the selection
#     accounts for EXACTLY the field count, so the two arms partition it;
#   * `order_is_input_not_argument` REFUTES the reading that fields come out in
#     the order the `-f` argument listed them (`-f3,1` prints field 1 first):
#     the output follows the input, which is why the node is a filtered sublist
#     and not a per-selector concatenation;
#   * `select_is_not_a_take` refutes reading a range as a prefix of the fields.
# `output_delimiter` is modelled as `intercalate`: the delimiter goes BETWEEN
# the selected fields, so `splice_single` says a one-field selection carries no
# delimiter at all (`output_delimiter_is_not_appended` refutes appending one) and
# `splice_three` pins the interleaving. The whitespace split is a different
# node's meaning on purpose (ws_split_would_lose_empty_field).
# BOUNDARY, proved: this is ONE delimiter byte (`split_on` takes a `nat`), not an
# IFS character SET, and the field text is taken literally — quoting, classes and
# brace alternation are outside the fragment (TextOps#fragment_is_three_constructors).
